<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulugh Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1302">1302</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulugh Khan was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1299, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilavada</a> and the temple of Somnath and forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghela</a> king Karna to flee. He also defeated a Mongol invasion in 1298 and took part in the siege of Ranthambore in 1301, dying soon afterwards in 1302.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulugh Khan was originally named Almas Beg. His father, Shihabuddin Mas'ud, was the elder brother of Sultan Jalaluddin, founder of the Khalji dynasty, and both Almas Beg and his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> married daughters of Jalaluddin. Under Jalaluddin he held the post of Akhur-beg, equivalent to master of the horse. In 1296, after Alauddin had raided Devagiri and kept the loot, Almas Beg assured the sultan of his brother's loyalty and persuaded him to visit Kara, where Alauddin killed him on 20 July 1296. The new sultan made his brother barbeg with the title Ulugh Khan, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>. He also held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>' of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In November 1296 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> sent him and Zafar Khan with an army of 30,000 to 40,000 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, held by Jalaluddin's son Arkali Khan. After two months of siege the leaders of the defence went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Ulugh Khan promised Arkali Khan and his brother Ruknuddin Ibrahim that they would not be harmed, but imprisoned them once the city was occupied; they were later blinded or killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> on Alauddin's orders. According to the courtier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a>, he reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej River</a> on 6 February 1298 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> there, with 20,000 Mongol dead.</p><p>On the way back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1299, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a>, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> punished soldiers for withholding the khums, the fifth of the loot due to the treasury. Recently converted Mongol soldiers mutinied; some 2,000 to 3,000 attacked his camp and killed a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> whom they took for him. Against Hammira of Ranthambore in 1301 he defeated and killed the general Bhimasimha at the Banas River, but after Nusrat Khan was killed in the siege he was forced back to Jhain, and Alauddin came in person and took the fort in July 1301.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ordered to prepare a march on the Kakatiya capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>, he assembled a large army but died a few months later. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziauddin-barani">Ziauddin Barani</a> writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> was greatly depressed and gave much in charity for his brother's soul. Barani's contemporary Isami claims instead that Alauddin had him poisoned, suspecting that he had been ready to take the throne when the sultan was wrongly reported dead; later chroniclers repeat this. The historian Banarsi Prasad Saksena dismisses Isami's account as false, while Kishori Saran Lal thinks it not impossible. Claims that he led the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1304, or governed it for 20 years, are of doubtful accuracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulugh Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Returning with reinforcements from Delhi after a failed first siege, <b>Ulugh Khan</b>, the future sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq, defeated and captured Prataparudra at the Kakatiya capital, Warangal.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1299 Alauddin Khalji's generals <b>Ulugh Khan</b> and Nusrat Khan invaded Gujarat and sacked Anahilavada; Karna fled and his queen Kamala Devi was taken to Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaghela-dynasty">Vaghela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
